# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT
## Open-Set Person Re-Identification (RE-ID) Web Application
### PS-1: Open-Set Person Re-Identification Under Low Inter-Class Variance

## 1. YOUR ROLE

Act as a senior computer vision engineer, machine learning researcher, AI architect, backend engineer, and full-stack web developer.

Design, implement, test, and document a complete, functional, research-oriented Open-Set Person Re-Identification (RE-ID) web application.

This is not merely a CCTV monitoring dashboard or a person detection application. The core objective is to develop an identity matching system that can distinguish enrolled workers from previously unseen people while minimizing false identity matches when different people have nearly identical appearances, especially when they wear the same uniforms.

Develop a genuine end-to-end application. Do not stop at architecture diagrams, static UI, mock detections, placeholder APIs, or unimplemented model interfaces.

Before making major architectural changes, inspect the existing repository, identify the current implementation and constraints, and preserve working functionality wherever practical.

## 2. PROBLEM STATEMENT

Implement the following research problem:

"Open-Set Person Re-Identification Under Low Inter-Class Variance."

Real-world surveillance systems must re-identify people who leave a camera's field of view and return later, or who appear in different cameras. The system must operate without a fixed number of identities.

The primary challenge is low inter-class appearance variance: different people may share the same uniform, batch, clothing color, body shape, and similar visual characteristics.

The most serious error is a false accept, where the system incorrectly assigns an unenrolled person or a different enrolled person to an existing identity.

The system must prioritize reliable identity matching and unknown rejection over forcing every observation into an existing identity.

### Primary research objective

Minimize false-accept rate at a specified true-accept rate or recall, with special evaluation on visually similar people.

### Mandatory constraints

- Use one compact person ReID embedding backbone for the primary embedding pipeline.
- Support an open-set gallery with a variable number of enrolled identities.
- Support real-time video processing, subject to measured hardware capacity.
- Detect multiple people in a single frame, targeting 10–20 simultaneous people where hardware and video quality permit.
- Support both live CCTV streams and uploaded videos.
- Maintain identity consistency across frames and, where evidence supports it, across cameras and videos.
- Explicitly reject ambiguous matches.
- Evaluate performance on public benchmarks and a curated low-variance subset.
- Provide reproducible experiments, ablations, and performance measurements.

## 3. FOUR REQUIRED PERSON CATEGORIES

Every tracked observation must receive one of four visible categories.

### A. Official Worker — GREEN

Condition:
- The person matches a verified enrolled worker.
- The match passes the calibrated acceptance policy.
- The enrollment record is active and valid.

Display:
- Worker ID or staff code.
- Worker name only if authorized.
- Batch, department, or uniform category.
- Camera ID.
- Current track ID.
- Match confidence and observation quality.
- First-seen and last-seen timestamps.

### B. New Person — BLUE

Condition:
- The observation is sufficiently clear for an identity decision.
- No enrolled identity passes the acceptance policy.
- The observation satisfies the configured policy for creating a provisional identity.

Behavior:
- Create a provisional person record or candidate identity.
- Associate it with a track and first-seen event.
- Save a representative crop only if permitted by the configured retention policy.
- Do not automatically declare the person a verified new individual across all cameras merely because one observation failed to match.

If the observation is not sufficiently clear, classify it as Unknown instead.

### C. Previously Enrolled Person — YELLOW

Condition:
- The observation confidently matches an existing enrolled identity.
- The identity is valid but the event represents a repeat observation or passage.

Behavior:
- Reuse the existing identity.
- Do not create a duplicate person.
- Save a new passage event.
- Increment repeat-passage counters.
- Store a quality-approved representative crop when appropriate.

Official workers can also be returning people. Model these concepts separately: identity/enrollment status and passage status. Use the four requested colors in the UI, with an explicit precedence policy for displaying them.

### D. Unknown / Uncertain — RED

Condition:
- No candidate passes the acceptance threshold.
- The top two candidate identities are too close in score.
- The crop is too small, blurred, occluded, or otherwise unsuitable.
- The available evidence is insufficient for a safe decision.
- The candidate match fails another required validation rule.

Behavior:
- Do not assign an existing identity.
- Do not automatically create a new identity.
- Record the observation as unresolved when permitted.
- Allow authorized review and correction.
- Re-evaluate when better evidence becomes available.

Important: Unknown does not necessarily mean new. It means that the system cannot safely match that observation to the enrolled gallery.

