## 13. DATASETS AND TRAINING

Use public person ReID datasets where licensing and access conditions permit.

Initial benchmark candidates:
- Market-1501.
- MSMT17.
- Other appropriate datasets identified after inspecting licensing, camera diversity, annotations, and task fit.

Do not assume that benchmark performance transfers to a workplace using nearly identical uniforms.

Create a curated low-variance evaluation subset with different identities wearing similar clothing. Use authorized data collection, clear annotation guidelines, and separate identity sets for training, calibration, and testing.

The low-variance subset should include:
- Same-uniform hard negatives.
- Similar clothing and body shape.
- Different camera viewpoints.
- Different lighting conditions.
- Partial occlusion.
- Low-resolution crops.
- Returning enrolled identities.
- Genuinely unenrolled identities.

Report dataset composition and limitations. Avoid leakage of the same identities across the train, calibration, and test partitions.

Do not claim results on a benchmark that has not actually been evaluated.

## 14. EVALUATION AND ABLATIONS

The evaluation pipeline must be reproducible and generate saved reports.

Required metrics:
- ROC curves.
- False-accept rate versus true-accept rate.
- False-accept rate at specified recall or true-accept-rate operating points.
- Open-set identification metrics appropriate to the protocol.
- Precision and recall for person detection.
- Tracking metrics where suitable.
- Identity-switch and track-fragmentation statistics.
- Inference latency.
- Throughput in frames per second.
- Peak memory use.
- Calibration metrics where applicable.

Run these ablations:

A. Baseline embedding with cosine threshold.

B. Baseline plus hard-negative mining.

C. Baseline plus margin-based metric learning.

D. Baseline plus image-quality-aware rejection.

E. Baseline plus calibrated score and margin-based rejection.

F. Baseline plus temporal aggregation.

G. Combined proposed approach.

For every experiment, document:
- Dataset and split.
- Model checkpoint.
- Training configuration.
- Matching threshold.
- Evaluation protocol.
- Hardware.
- Random seed.
- Results.
- Known limitations.

Compare methods at the same target recall or true-accept rate. Never compare false-accept rates at unrelated operating points and claim that one method is better.

Generate plots and tables suitable for a technical research report.

